---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../lib/ui';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, MIN_AGE, TERMS_UPDATED, TERMS_VERSION, contactHref } from '../lib/terms';

// Everything here should match what the code actually stores. Check it again whenever storage changes.
const mailto = contactHref();
---

<Layout title="Privacy" description={`What ${SITE_NAME} stores about you, who can see it, and how long it's kept.`}>
  <div class="container page" style="max-width: 760px">
    <div class="page-head">
      <div class="stack" style="--gap: 8px">
        <span class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</span>
        <h1 class="display">What we keep, and why</h1>
        <p class="muted lede">Only what the game needs to work. Here’s all of it.</p>
        <p class="faint small">Version {TERMS_VERSION} · Updated {TERMS_UPDATED}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <article class="stack doc" style="--gap: 20px">
      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We never ask for your email address, real name, phone number or payment details.</li>
          <li>No ads, no analytics, no tracking cookies.</li>
          <li>We don’t sell or rent your data, or share it with anyone beyond what it takes to run the site.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">In this policy, “we” means the people who run {SITE_NAME}.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>What we store</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt>Your account</dt>
          <dd>
            Your username, your display name, when you signed up, and which version of the Terms and this policy you agreed
            to, and when. Your password is stored only as a salted scrypt hash, so nobody, us included, can read it back.
          </dd>

          <dt>Your sign-ins</dt>
          <dd>
            Signing in puts a random token in a cookie in your browser. We keep only a SHA-256 hash of that token, which
            account it belongs to, and when it expires. A sign-in lasts 60 days and is extended while you keep using the site.
            Signing out deletes it, and changing your password signs out your other devices. We don’t record your device or
            location with it.
          </dd>

          <dt>Your games</dt>
          <dd>
            Each game’s name, join code and host, who sits in which seat, the house rules and table settings, and the full
            state of the game: every card dealt, passed and played, including the crib. Every finished hand is saved too
            (its tricks, points and scores), along with when things happened, because the scoreboard and stats are worked out
            from them. Games are kept until the host deletes them.
          </dd>

          <dt>Chat</dt>
          <dd>
            What you write in a game’s chat, who wrote it and when. At 24 hours old a message stops showing, and it’s deleted
            the next time anyone uses chat (or with its game, if that goes first). Nobody can delete one by hand, not even
            whoever wrote it. Which messages you’ve read, and whether chat bubbles are switched off, are kept only in your
            browser.
          </dd>

          <dt>Your profile photo</dt>
          <dd>
            Only if you add one. Your browser crops it to a small square before it’s uploaded. The server then checks it,
            strips its metadata (camera details, location, comments) and stores it in the site’s database. When you replace or
            remove your photo, the old one is deleted from the site, though browsers that already showed it may keep a copy in
            their cache.
          </dd>

          <dt>Password reset links</dt>
          <dd>
            If a site admin makes you a reset link, we store a hash of it, who made it, and when it expires. Each link works
            once, for 24 hours.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>IP addresses</h2>
        <p>
          To slow down password guessing, the server counts sign-in, sign-up and password-reset attempts by IP address, and
          sign-in attempts by username. Those counts are held only in the server’s memory for up to 15 minutes. They’re never
          written to the database, and they’re forgotten whenever the server restarts. The site doesn’t keep a log of
          visitors’ IP addresses.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Who can see what</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Players at your tables</strong> see your display name, username and photo, your plays and scores, and
            whether you have the game open. They never see the cards in your hand: the server only sends each player their own.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The players seated at a game</strong> can read its chat, and nobody else. Someone the host takes out of a
            game can’t read it any more, but what they wrote stays, under their name, until it’s 24 hours old.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Anyone signed in with a game’s join code</strong> can see the game’s name and who’s sitting at it before
            they join.
          </li>
          <li><strong>People you’ve shared a table with</strong> see your stats from the games you played together.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your photo</strong> can be seen by anyone signed in who has its web address. Each upload gets a new,
            unguessable address. Photos are never shown to people who aren’t signed in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Site admins</strong> can see the list of accounts (usernames, display names, sign-up dates, how many devices
            are signed in, and whether a reset link is waiting) and can remove a photo.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The people who run the site</strong> have access to the server, so they can see everything listed on this
            page.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">We’d only hand anything to anyone else if the law required it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Cookies and your browser</h2>
        <p>The site sets two cookies, both its own:</p>
        <dl>
          <dt><code>hearts_session</code></dt>
          <dd>Keeps you signed in, for up to 60 days. The page’s scripts can’t read it.</dd>
          <dt><code>hearts_deck</code></dt>
          <dd>Remembers whether you picked the two-color or four-color deck, for a year.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          Your browser also keeps a few small settings of its own, such as whether turn alerts are on and which hand results
          you’ve already seen. Those stay on your device and are never sent to us. Turn alerts use your browser’s notification
          permission, which we don’t store either.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">There are no advertising or analytics cookies, which is why there’s no cookie banner.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Other services</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt>Fly.io (hosting)</dt>
          <dd>
            The site and its database run on Fly.io, on servers in the United States. Every visit passes through Fly.io’s
            network, so Fly.io sees your IP address, and its own privacy policy covers that.
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="muted">That’s the full list. No ad networks, analytics, social buttons or trackers.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Backups</h2>
        <p>
          Fly.io takes daily snapshots of the disk the database lives on and keeps them for a few days. Something you delete,
          like an old photo or a deleted game, can stay in those snapshots until they expire.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Your choices</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Change your display name and password, or add or remove your photo, on the <a href="/account">Account</a> page.</li>
          <li>
            To delete your account, or to ask for a copy of what we hold about you, get in touch (see Contact below).
            There’s no delete button yet, so it’s done by hand. Games belong to everyone who played in them, so their score
            history may be kept for the other players.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          The site isn’t meant for anyone under {MIN_AGE}. If you think someone under {MIN_AGE} has an account, let us know and
          we’ll delete it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes in a way that matters, its version number goes up and you’ll be asked to agree again the next
          time you open the site, just like the <a href="/terms">Terms</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="panel panel-pad">
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, or want something deleted? {mailto ? <>Email us at <a href={mailto}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</> : 'Ask a site admin, or whoever invited you to the site.'}
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
    max-width: 620px;
  }

  .small {
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }

  .doc {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .doc section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .doc h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 560;
    font-size: 22px;
  }

  .doc p,
  .doc li,
  .doc dd {
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .doc ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .doc dl {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .doc dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .doc dt:not(:first-child) {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }

  .doc dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .doc code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
